<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleni of Ethiopia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1522">1522</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Empress Eleni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleni was an Ethiopian empress, born a princess of the Muslim Hadiya Sultanate, who married Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. She remained a powerful figure at court under several later emperors and acted as regent for the young Lebna Dengel. Fearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, she sent the envoy Mateus to Portugal around 1509 to seek an alliance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleni-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="Eleni of Ethiopia" width="300" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, but died over 70 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleni_of_Hadiya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
